In message #93-8-222, Nick Szabo <szabo@netcom.com> wrote:

> My purpose was not to attack Browne in particular, but to critique pop
> strategies for individual investors in general, and point out fallacies
> that  are repeated over and over again in that literature.

Well and good. There are many real frauds out there.

> but expecting such a simple scheme to generate a greater return
> than the overall market,

I interpret this differently. This looks to me more like evidence that
you can get a 5% return from the market, after inflation, without taking
undue risk. This is not "beating the market"... it's just showing a way
you can maximize your market returns. I don't know where your 2.5%
figure came from, but it seems suspect to me. I know it's not
money-market returns... those are negative right now.

>> But I'm not *picking* anything.

> Yes, you are.  The 5%/year claim is an explicit claim that by
> picking certain securities, one can beat the market.

Only if you believe that "the market" yields only 2.5% in real terms.

> I assume then you can tell us, what are the typical bid/ask spreads
> on gold coins?   How do they vary with market volume?  Does market
> volume or the bid/ask spread vary in any predictable way (eg
> seasonally)?  Which markets have the lowest and highest bid/ask
> spreads for the individual investor?

Actually, I can't. As I explained, I'm unlikely to buy or sell more than
5% (2 or 3%, really) of my portfolio in the form of gold coins in any
given year, so I don't track it that closely. If I were buying or
selling 25% or 50% of my portfolio in a year, these transactions costs
would be *much* more significant. I record the price at which I buy and
the price at which I sell, and the spread is reflected in these and in
any calculations I make of performance. I shop around for quotes: when I
want to buy, I buy whereever is cheapest, when I want to sell, I sell
whereever is dearest. (Of course, prices have to be calculated to
include the costs of any shipping, insurance, or travel to avoid
California's insane sales tax on gold purchases of less than $1000.)
Since I almost always buy to hold for at least a full year, I don't look
at spreads per se.

The general impression I have is that spreads can run as high as 5% on
the smaller, fractional-ounce coins, if you aren't careful. But if you
always buy at the place that quotes the lowest price and sell at the
place that quotes the highest, you'll get the smallest spreads. At
retail these are in the region of 1.5% to 3%, depending on recent price
volatility. Big prices moves make for big spreads, stable prices make
for small ones. The effect of current market volatility on the spread
always outweighs the effect of seasonality, which market you choose, or
the form into which the gold is cast (except that, of course, the
smaller coins have bigger spreads). So you can avoid high spreads by
only buying or selling during periods of relatively stable prices.

