In message #93-8-230, Hal Finney <hfinney@shell.portal.com> wrote:

> Harry Browne's ideas are used in a particular mutual fund called the
> "Permanent Portfolio".  You can find prices listed every day in the
> newspaper.

This fund, which I mentioned (not by name) in an earlier post, reflects
his ideas as of the late Seventies/early Eighties. He doesn't seem to
have changed his recommendations much since the mid-Eighties, but he has
since the original Permanent Portfolio fund was established. The PP Fund
as it currently exists is overweighted in inflation hedges and
underweighted in stocks and bonds, which is why its performance
throughout the Eighties left something to be desired.

However, the same company also offers an excellent money-market fund
called (imaginatively enough) the Treasury Bill Portfolio, which is
unusual among money-market funds in that it has a variable (steadily
increasing) share value. This allows you to protect income from taxation
by treating it as a capital gain... which you can postpone taking for as
many years as you like, while the interest compounds. There are legal
limits on how much of this they can get away while still qualifying as
an untaxed investment company, but it's fund policy to get away with as
much of this as they can. Other money-market funds don't even bother;
they give you all the interest as income immediately.

They also have a Variable Bond Portfolio, which invests in upper-tier
investment-grade corporate bonds to mature in two years or less, and an
Aggressive Growth Portfolio, which is all stocks and some stock
warrants.

All three of these funds are much more useful than the company's
original product. I think their number is 800-531-5142.

The Benham Group (800-4SAFETY) also has products that are useful in
implementing this strategy, namely their zero-coupon T-bond funds and a
low-spread gold brokerage.

>Eric Watt Forste wrote:
>> People who keep gold in a
>> safe deposit box at a bank might as well just sell it and put the
>> proceeds into a money-market fund.

> I don't think this is completely true, as gold provides a hedge against
