Date: Tue, 10 Aug 1993 19:06:04 -0700
From: rpeck@pure.com  (Ray Peck)
Subject: ENVIRON: Cutting down our (old growth) forests

Extropians-Request@gnu.ai.mit.edu writes:
>From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
>
>Could someone please tell me what the problem is with cutting down our
>forests so long as we replant them? 

Well, I for one have little problem with the tree farms that we grow
and cut down.  I think it's damaging to clearcut them, due to topsoil
loss (and general ugliness), but other than clearcutting I have no
problem with that.

I do have a problem with cutting the "remaining 5%".  We can replant
them, yes, but we can't restore them to what they were, not for
thousands of years.  And cutting the last 5% will only get us enough
"farm space" for another couple years of usage growth.  What do we do
then?  And why not do that now?

>And if the "95% of old growth
>forest has been cut down" statistic is true, why isn't there plenty of
>land to use for tree farming? It would seem that the remaining 5%
>might not be worth cutting.

I agree.  Seems to me that a large part of the problem is government,
again.  An example: water subsidies reward people for planting alfalfa
and rice in semi-desert areas of California.  Were those subsidies not
available, perhaps the land would more profitably used for planting
pulp trees, which use much less water/unit product.

Another example.  The forestry service sells a large part of the
"national forest's" wood at a loss to the taxpayer (they spend more
building roads for the loggers than they get for the wood).  Without
us paying subsidies to the wood industry, the price of virgin wood
would raise, and we'd see a drop in the price of recycled pulp,
because more people would be using it.


