From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
Subject: axioms: Existence exists.  Yes, and?

I may be coming in a bit late in the day.  Well, anyway, quoted lines
are from Tim Starr, until otherwise noted, over the past week or so. 
Sorry about the scatteredness.

> Very well.  Take, for example, the axiom: "existence exists."

First of all, by "existence" do you mean the sum of things that exist,
or the quality they have in common?  If the former, are "things" all nouns
or only the concrete?  (Is the existence of a quality separable from the
existence of objects with that quality?)

> What is its context?  What system does it "generate"?

Well, what are its consequences?

>> Existence is a definition.
> No, it isn't.  A definition consists of a genus and a differentia.
> "Existence" has neither.

Genus:  Quality.  Differentia:  Common to all things.
(How do we tell a genus from a coarser differentia?)

> He asks for a test of a metaphysical axiom.  It's the same for the test
> of any axiom: try to deny it without presuming it.  If you can, it's
> not an axiom.  If you can't, then it is.

Not obvious to me what "presuming" means -- are you disallowing
reductio ad absurdum?

>>  Occam's razor.
> That's logic, not aesthetics.

Eh?  There is even less _logical_ justification for Occam than
for induction; both are empirical heuristics.

> If nothing existed, this would include the illusion that anything
> existed.  If the illusion exists, then existence must exist.  This denial
> presupposes what it denies.  Thus, your test proves that the axiom holds.

So, at least an illusion of existence must exist.  Big deal!
(And that's assuming an illusion can be said to exist.)

 ~ ~ ~
Sean Merritt adds:
> (and yes A = A is a tautology and not an axiom!)

As part of a definition of "=", it is a postulate.

 ~ ~ ~
Ray:
>  Imaginary numbers are not really an axiom but a definition (in
> terms of other axioms). The imaginary number 'i' simply means
> "let this symbol represent the quantity square_root(-1)"

Or, operationally, "let i*i == -1"; this leaves open what, if anything, i *is*.  (In the quaternion system, -1 has six distinct square roots instead of two.)

