Except for the strawman of extreme realism, there's nothing contrary to
reason in this.  See "The Objectivist Ethics" by Rand for a good example
of a naturalistic derivation of good and bad.

>This viewpoint holds that the source of "The Good" and "The Bad" is
>the set of rules. Where do the rules come from? "The nature of
>things", i.e. they are revealed truth, whether or not that revelation
>id done by god or not.

Bullshit.  They are no more revealed truth than the laws of economics.

>There is no reason involved. No one asks where
>"the good" and "the bad" really come from -- although some noises are
>made to that effect, just as Aquinas made noises to the effect of
>proving god by logical means.

Aquinas made 5 arguments for the existence of God which failed, and are
generally recognized as doing so.  In order for the analogy to work, there
would have to be arguments for the existence of right and wrong which have
failed.  I submit the abovementioned work by Rand as one which does not
fail.  Rand DOES ask what the origin of good and bad are.

What's more, her work is largely within and based on the Aristotelian
tradition, in which life (the life of an individual) is the standard of
value, value is that which is life-supporting, good actions are those that
are life-supporting, and bad ones are those that are harmful to life.

Aquinas based his secular ethics (as opposed to his theological ones; he
maintained a strict distinction between the two) upon the foundational
axiom: "Do good, avoid evil."  Mortimer Adler formulates it as: "we ought
to desire those things that are truly good for us."

>Now, there is another viewpoint, which is this: we define the good and
>the bad based simply on our own goals, which are arational.

...

>Note
>that there is no question of "Good" and "Bad" here being absolute --
>we recognise that we have defined those categories based on what we
>think will promote or fail to promote our goals, and those categories
>may be changed as our information changes and as our goals change. In
>other words, there is no "law" here, only that which we have made.

Goal-oriented behavior is called "teleological."  You present a teleological
approach to ethics.  So does the Aristotelian tradition.  The ultimate end
of an individual's life is the end and standard by which conduct is judged.

The difference is that in the Aristotelian tradition individuals that can
live ought to do so categorically, without exception.  They are obligated
to base their conduct upon the standard of this ultimate end.  There is
some controversy about whether this means that all conduct must be
instrumental to life, or that some or all conduct must be "constitutive" of
this ultimate end, but the basic principle is sound.

The other mistake you seem to be making is assuming that natural law ethics
consists of a set of rules to be followed in all particular circumstances,
a la Kantian deontological ethics.  This is one of the mistakes of Modern
ethics: rule-based morality, rather than standard-based as was Classical
ethics and as the Aristotelian tradition as it survives in Thomism and
Objectivism has it.

>I object to any notion of absolute good and bad.

So, such a notion is absolutely bad?  No, of course not, just relative to
your own personal goal-set, which seems to be based upon living...

As for what "most people" call morality, the Christian ethics that Western
civilization is largely, although not completely, based upon is flawed.  Not
for its "absolute" character, however, but for its altruism.

