>First of all, by "existence" do you mean the sum of things that exist,
>or the quality they have in common?  If the former, are "things" all nouns
>or only the concrete?  (Is the existence of a quality separable from the
>existence of objects with that quality?)

The former.  I prefer the term "characteristics" to "qualities."  Character-
istics do not exist apart from the things they are characteristics of.

The primary metaphysical categories of Objectivism are: entity, attribute,
characteristic, identity.  An entity is a thing.  Attributes are "of"
entities, that is, secondary "things" that entities "have," such as
extension or location.  Characteristics are attributes that entities have
in common, or that an entity has in common with itself over time and maybe
place, I don't quite recall.  The sum of an entity's attributes (or maybe
it's the sum of its characteristics, I don't quite recall right now - it's
been a while, I'd have to check to be sure) constitutes its identity.

Attributes are logically separable from entities, but not metaphysically.

